course: dictionary
lesson: 5239
title: Stretchy words for time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words measure time exactly, like a second or an hour. Others stretch. «I'll be back in a jiffy» means very soon, a short moment. Nobody checks a jiffy with a clock, so its length is a guess.
explain: Look at the words around jiffy. «Just a jiffy» and «in half a jiffy» both tell you it will be quick. The word «in» shows that the waiting is still to come, so the speaker means soon, not now.
explain: Words like «a tick» and «a mo» also mean a short moment. Be careful, though: «back in a jiffy» once took 12 minutes! In the speaker's mind a jiffy is quick, but in real life it can be longer.
explain: Jiffy has a second meaning in the UK. A Jiffy bag is a padded envelope for posting things. Check the sentence to see which meaning fits: is the talk about waiting, or about posting?
fact: A jiffy is a very short time, a moment.
fact: Jiffy is informal, so it is chatty language that you hear more in talk and stories than in formal writing.
fact: «In a jiffy» means very soon, as in «I'll be back in a jiffy».
fact: A Jiffy bag, with a capital J, is a padded envelope for posting things (UK).
fact: Other stretchy time words include «a tick», «a mo» and «two shakes», which all mean a short moment.
exercise: «I'll be back in a jiffy.» What does in a jiffy mean? | Never | At midnight | Next week | *Very soon
exercise: Which word means a short moment, like jiffy? | Forever | *A tick | Yesterday | Decade
exercise: Ella finds a Jiffy bag on the doormat. What is it most likely to be? | A very slow snail | A long walk | *A padded envelope for posting things | A sort of dog
test: Mum says, «Just a jiffy, I'm nearly ready.» What does she mean? | Go away for a whole day | Sleep until morning | *Wait a very short time | Stop talking forever
test: Why is a jiffy called a stretchy word? | It always means a very long time | It is measured exactly by a clock | It is always exactly one minute | *Nobody measures it, and it can take longer than expected
exam: Why might a speaker say «in a jiffy» instead of «in exactly one minute»? | It is more formal and gives an exact time | It means the same as a year | It is a Jiffy bag | *It sounds friendly and relaxed, and no exact time is promised
